1
00:00:03,220 --> 00:00:05,179
You take the south side.
I'll take north.

2
00:00:05,266 --> 00:00:07,703
You met the new boss yet?
I heard he's young.

3
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:09,966
Watch for any potential
threats to the mayor.

4
00:00:10,053 --> 00:00:12,273
Younger than us, and he's got
limited field experience.

5
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:14,797
[Lisbon]
'Mayor just left the building.
The march is underway.'

6
00:00:14,884 --> 00:00:16,451
Copy that.
Moving into position.

7
00:00:16,538 --> 00:00:19,062
Doesn't it bother you, working
with a barely qualified guy?

8
00:00:19,149 --> 00:00:21,456
You get used to it.

9
00:00:21,543 --> 00:00:24,763
[woman screams]
I've got an unknown disturbance
in the northeast alley.

10
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:26,200
- Excuse me. Police.
- CBI.

11
00:00:26,287 --> 00:00:27,897
- Coming through.
- Excuse me.

12
00:00:31,509 --> 00:00:33,946
- You see what happened?
- No. Lo vi en el suelo.

13
00:00:34,034 --> 00:00:36,688
Anybody see what happened?
Alguien vio lo que paso?

14
00:00:36,775 --> 00:00:38,995
Okay, I need for you
to secure the alley.

15
00:00:39,082 --> 00:00:41,519
Get backup in here now. Contact
the mayor's security detail.

16
00:00:41,606 --> 00:00:43,478
Clear these people out now.

17
00:00:44,783 --> 00:00:46,220
He's dead.

18
00:00:46,307 --> 00:00:48,657
- 'Excuse me.'
- 'Excuse me. Watch out.'

19
00:00:50,137 --> 00:00:51,399
What do we got?

20
00:00:51,486 --> 00:00:52,791
Homicide.
Multiple stab wounds.

21
00:00:52,878 --> 00:00:55,316
- Any ID?
- No wallet, nothing I can see.

22
00:00:55,403 --> 00:00:57,796
Got a large transient
population. Could be a robbery.

23
00:00:57,883 --> 00:01:00,190
- Inform the office.
- Sure.

24
00:01:00,277 --> 00:01:02,366
Jane.

25
00:01:02,453 --> 00:01:05,195
[indistinct shouting]
Speak up, Lisbon.
It's a little loud here.

26
00:01:05,282 --> 00:01:07,415
- Are you close to us?
- Well, that depends.

27
00:01:07,502 --> 00:01:08,938
Are you still on crowd patrol?

28
00:01:09,025 --> 00:01:10,635
No. We got a homicide now.

29
00:01:10,722 --> 00:01:13,943
Then I'm close.

30
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:16,772
[indistinct shouting]

31
00:01:21,255 --> 00:01:24,084
[distorted indistinct shouting]

32
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:45,844
[indistinct conversations]

33
00:01:46,889 --> 00:01:48,934
[siren wailing]

34
00:01:49,021 --> 00:01:50,936
[Lisbon]
'Hey, Jane!'

35
00:01:51,023 --> 00:01:53,722
Strange time for murder. In
the middle of a protest march?

36
00:01:53,809 --> 00:01:55,985
Leave it to a killer to do
something crazy.

37
00:01:56,072 --> 00:01:58,814
Or calculated.

38
00:01:58,901 --> 00:02:00,207
Notice how this alleyway

39
00:02:00,294 --> 00:02:02,600
is starting to look like
a policeman's ball?

40
00:02:02,687 --> 00:02:05,777
If I wanted to use a bullet
to express my displeasure

41
00:02:05,864 --> 00:02:08,476
with the mayor's politics,
I'd clear out the heat.

42
00:02:08,563 --> 00:02:10,304
You think this murder
is a decoy?

43
00:02:10,391 --> 00:02:12,088
No clue, but there's
a suspicious-looking man

44
00:02:12,175 --> 00:02:13,872
on "J" street and 56th.

45
00:02:13,959 --> 00:02:17,354
Haunted eyes, nervous energy,
an overcoat on a hot day.

46
00:02:17,441 --> 00:02:19,182
That's thin, even for you.

47
00:02:19,269 --> 00:02:21,706
Yeah, you're probably right.

48
00:02:21,793 --> 00:02:24,579
It's only the mayor's life
at stake. Forget I spoke.

49
00:02:29,018 --> 00:02:29,975
Cho.

50
00:02:31,412 --> 00:02:33,414
[indistinct conversations]

51
00:02:33,501 --> 00:02:35,894
[chanting in Spanish]

52
00:02:48,690 --> 00:02:49,908
We got him.
Southeast corner.

53
00:02:49,995 --> 00:02:52,781
[chanting in Spanish continues]

54
00:02:57,786 --> 00:03:00,484
CBI. Put your hands
where I can see 'em.

55
00:03:00,571 --> 00:03:03,574
Do me a favor and step
over here with me.

56
00:03:03,661 --> 00:03:05,663
What's the problem, ma'am?
I didn't do anything.

57
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:08,449
Then you won't mind
if we search you.

58
00:03:08,536 --> 00:03:10,233
Hands on your head,
interlace your fingers.

59
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:11,756
Turn around
and face the wall.

60
00:03:11,843 --> 00:03:13,193
[chanting in Spanish continues]

61
00:03:16,196 --> 00:03:17,806
Gun.

62
00:03:17,893 --> 00:03:19,503
A semiautomatic .45.

63
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:21,723
[clicks]
Fully loaded.

64
00:03:21,810 --> 00:03:23,638
[click]

65
00:03:23,725 --> 00:03:25,814
Chambered round.

66
00:03:25,901 --> 00:03:27,468
I can explain that.

67
00:03:27,555 --> 00:03:29,426
Station's right around
the corner. Turn around.

68
00:03:29,513 --> 00:03:30,427
[handcuffs click]

69
00:03:33,169 --> 00:03:34,126
Come on.

70
00:03:34,214 --> 00:03:36,216
[chanting in Spanish continues]

71
00:03:38,957 --> 00:03:40,307
[theme music]

72
00:03:50,882 --> 00:03:54,408
That's a concealed carry license
and that's my name. Henry Tibbs.

73
00:03:54,495 --> 00:03:57,324
Gives me authority to carry my
sidearm anywhere in California.

74
00:03:57,411 --> 00:03:58,977
These aren't easy
to come by.

75
00:03:59,064 --> 00:04:00,979
You had to demonstrate
a need to carry.

76
00:04:01,066 --> 00:04:03,112
I'm a Texas man.

77
00:04:03,199 --> 00:04:04,679
That's my need.

78
00:04:04,766 --> 00:04:07,072
Met a local sheriff
who sympathized.

79
00:04:07,159 --> 00:04:08,422
Hook 'em horns.

80
00:04:08,509 --> 00:04:10,032
Why were you standing there

81
00:04:10,119 --> 00:04:11,425
with your legal
concealed sidearm?

82
00:04:11,512 --> 00:04:14,428
I was, uh, downtown,
dropping off resumes.

83
00:04:14,515 --> 00:04:16,908
I'm, uh, an insurance salesman.

84
00:04:16,995 --> 00:04:18,649
I've been out of work.

85
00:04:18,736 --> 00:04:21,304
The economy's tighter
than bark on a tree.

86
00:04:21,391 --> 00:04:23,654
You always go job hunting
with a loaded gun?

87
00:04:23,741 --> 00:04:26,831
There were several hundred
angry protesters on that street.

88
00:04:26,918 --> 00:04:29,921
And you put yourself
right in the middle of 'em.

89
00:04:30,008 --> 00:04:33,360
Sure. It's not every day
you get to see a big-city mayor.

90
00:04:34,361 --> 00:04:35,666
[Cho]
'Or shoot him.'

91
00:04:35,753 --> 00:04:38,147
Henry Tibbs.
Clean record, no priors.

92
00:04:38,234 --> 00:04:39,757
Gun permit checks out.

93
00:04:39,844 --> 00:04:41,846
Says here
he's on unemployment

94
00:04:41,933 --> 00:04:43,631
owns a home
with his wife Linda

95
00:04:43,718 --> 00:04:45,110
but they're behind
on payments.

96
00:04:45,197 --> 00:04:48,157
And the sun came up
just to hear him crow

97
00:04:48,244 --> 00:04:49,637
as they say in Texas.

98
00:04:49,724 --> 00:04:51,421
Do we have anything
that connects Tibbs

99
00:04:51,508 --> 00:04:53,249
to our John Doe
in the alley?

100
00:04:53,336 --> 00:04:55,033
Not yet. Forensics swabbed him
for bloodstains.

101
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:56,121
Couldn't find anything.

102
00:04:56,208 --> 00:04:58,298
I don't like what I'm hearing.

103
00:04:58,385 --> 00:05:00,561
Police put out an alert
for a hate crime

104
00:05:00,648 --> 00:05:03,955
so we rounded up
the nearest white guy.

105
00:05:04,042 --> 00:05:06,262
And while that's a refreshing
twist on racial profiling

106
00:05:06,349 --> 00:05:08,656
Mr. Tibbs has himself
grounds for a lawsuit.

107
00:05:08,743 --> 00:05:11,398
We should let him go
and send his wife some flowers.

108
00:05:13,704 --> 00:05:16,968
Special agent Luther Wainright.
I'm your new boss.

109
00:05:17,055 --> 00:05:19,057
Welcome aboard.
I'm Agent Lisbon.

110
00:05:19,144 --> 00:05:20,581
Agent Lisbon,
nice to meet you.

111
00:05:20,668 --> 00:05:22,147
- This is Agent Rig--
- Agent Rigsby.

112
00:05:22,234 --> 00:05:24,715
- Pleasure to meet you.
- Van Pelt.

113
00:05:24,802 --> 00:05:27,414
I've read everyone's file,
so I kind of know..

114
00:05:27,501 --> 00:05:30,678
Patrick Jane. Still making
my way through yours.

115
00:05:30,765 --> 00:05:32,244
It's a little bit
heavier reading.

116
00:05:32,332 --> 00:05:35,422
Yeah, do me a favor.
Don't tell me how it ends.

117
00:05:35,509 --> 00:05:37,249
[chuckles]

118
00:05:37,337 --> 00:05:40,775
Uh, hard times, everyone, um,
for the entire unit

119
00:05:40,862 --> 00:05:43,647
but, uh, things are about to get
a whole lot easier.

120
00:05:43,734 --> 00:05:47,477
I pride myself on two things,
trust and transparency.

121
00:05:47,564 --> 00:05:49,740
Excellent, so you'll
tell us how old you are?

122
00:05:49,827 --> 00:05:51,525
Rigsby here is running
an office pool.

123
00:05:51,612 --> 00:05:53,701
I'm not running a pool.

124
00:05:53,788 --> 00:05:55,703
Henry Tibbs. What's the plan?

125
00:05:55,790 --> 00:05:58,793
I suggest that we release him
and keep him under surveillance.

126
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:01,622
Yeah, that sounds reasonable.
Keep me posted.

127
00:06:01,709 --> 00:06:04,929
Oh, um, your John Doe,
he may be a photographer.

128
00:06:05,016 --> 00:06:08,019
On long shoot days, that bandage
on the bridge of his nose

129
00:06:08,106 --> 00:06:10,065
would keep the nose pads
on his eyeglasses

130
00:06:10,152 --> 00:06:11,327
from digging into his skin.

131
00:06:11,414 --> 00:06:12,546
We'll check it out.

132
00:06:12,633 --> 00:06:14,243
Excellent. Thank you.

133
00:06:16,463 --> 00:06:17,377
Sharp.

134
00:06:24,906 --> 00:06:26,864
This is the surveillance car
you chose?

135
00:06:26,951 --> 00:06:29,606
We can't take an official
vehicle. We need to blend in.

136
00:06:29,693 --> 00:06:32,261
Okay, and, uh, this blends in?

137
00:06:32,348 --> 00:06:35,830
Nobody's forcing you
to go on this surveillance.

138
00:06:35,917 --> 00:06:38,267
You're right. Good point,
but I'm coming anyway.

139
00:06:38,354 --> 00:06:40,356
Did you bring a disguise?

140
00:06:45,492 --> 00:06:47,929
[car door opens]
"Did I bring a disguise?"

141
00:06:48,016 --> 00:06:49,583
So Wainright was spot-on.

142
00:06:49,670 --> 00:06:51,149
I checked
a professional directory

143
00:06:51,236 --> 00:06:53,238
for local photographers,
there he was. Scott Gibson.

144
00:06:53,325 --> 00:06:55,023
Spoke to the magazine
he works for.

145
00:06:55,110 --> 00:06:57,329
He was shooting
a photo essay of the homeless.

146
00:06:57,417 --> 00:06:59,114
- Do we know anything else?
- Clean record.

147
00:06:59,201 --> 00:07:00,985
Lives alone,
no family to speak of

148
00:07:01,072 --> 00:07:02,813
always carries a cell phone
on assignment.

149
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:05,599
There was no phone
at the crime scene or a camera.

150
00:07:05,686 --> 00:07:07,296
Your robbery theory
is looking better.

151
00:07:07,383 --> 00:07:09,820
Let's hope so. Got a GPS track
on Gibson's phone.

152
00:07:09,907 --> 00:07:11,169
Residential neighborhood,
south of here.

153
00:07:11,256 --> 00:07:12,475
Way to bury the lead.

154
00:07:12,562 --> 00:07:13,650
- Let's go.
- Okay.

155
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:22,354
[engine rumbling]

156
00:07:26,054 --> 00:07:27,664
[engine turns off]

157
00:07:27,751 --> 00:07:29,710
[Patrick sighs]
'Okay.'

158
00:07:31,625 --> 00:07:33,453
[exhales deeply]

159
00:07:33,540 --> 00:07:34,497
So what now?

160
00:07:34,584 --> 00:07:35,629
Now we wait.

161
00:07:35,716 --> 00:07:37,108
Wait for what?

162
00:07:37,195 --> 00:07:38,719
For Tibbs to come back out.

163
00:07:38,806 --> 00:07:40,982
That's no fun. I thought
the point of surveillance

164
00:07:41,069 --> 00:07:43,593
was to, uh, surveil.

165
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:45,943
It's hard to do that
from inside a car.

166
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:48,468
Well, I can't go in there.
Tibbs might recognize me.

167
00:07:48,555 --> 00:07:51,166
Mm. That is a problem.

168
00:07:51,253 --> 00:07:54,299
Well, he's never met me.
Problem solved.

169
00:07:54,386 --> 00:07:55,518
I'm gonna need these.

170
00:07:55,605 --> 00:07:56,519
Hey.

171
00:07:58,434 --> 00:07:59,391
Jane.

172
00:08:04,353 --> 00:08:07,138
[indistinct conversations]

173
00:08:13,623 --> 00:08:15,016
[southern accent]
Bourbon. Big one.

174
00:08:15,103 --> 00:08:18,367
Bigger than all hell
and half of Texas.

175
00:08:18,454 --> 00:08:20,500
A lone star man, huh?

176
00:08:20,587 --> 00:08:23,546
Yeah, what's it to ya, hoss?

177
00:08:23,633 --> 00:08:25,243
[chuckles]

178
00:08:28,595 --> 00:08:29,726
I seen you before.

179
00:08:29,813 --> 00:08:31,728
Yeah?

180
00:08:31,815 --> 00:08:33,469
Yeah. Let me think.

181
00:08:33,556 --> 00:08:40,084
"Soldier of fortune", weaponry
convention, Vegas, 2004.

182
00:08:40,171 --> 00:08:41,651
Nope.

183
00:08:41,738 --> 00:08:43,653
No? Well, that's too bad.

184
00:08:43,740 --> 00:08:45,568
That was full-auto
rock 'n' roll mayhem

185
00:08:45,655 --> 00:08:50,747
in the desert, my friend.
Uzis, floozies, and Jacuzzis.

186
00:08:50,834 --> 00:08:52,357
Weaponry convention, huh?

187
00:08:52,444 --> 00:08:53,881
Mm.

188
00:08:53,968 --> 00:08:56,623
One of the greatest weekends
of my life

189
00:08:56,710 --> 00:08:59,364
and I've had some weekends.

190
00:08:59,451 --> 00:09:03,020
No offense, pal. You don't look
like a weapons enthusiast.

191
00:09:03,107 --> 00:09:05,501
You wanna catch chickens,
you don't look like a fox.

192
00:09:05,588 --> 00:09:07,111
Am I right?

193
00:09:07,198 --> 00:09:08,635
Damn straight, I'm right.

194
00:09:11,376 --> 00:09:12,987
What's your weapon of choice?

195
00:09:13,074 --> 00:09:15,990
A loaded one.

196
00:09:16,077 --> 00:09:19,036
Can I get another
and one here for my friend?

197
00:09:31,092 --> 00:09:33,268
The cell phone signal came
from this house.

198
00:09:41,581 --> 00:09:44,496
Foreclosure property.
Door's ajar.

199
00:09:44,584 --> 00:09:45,889
[whispers]
You ready?

200
00:09:45,976 --> 00:09:47,325
[door creaks]

201
00:10:16,398 --> 00:10:18,705
Hey, back here.

202
00:10:18,792 --> 00:10:20,445
Hey, wake up.

203
00:10:20,532 --> 00:10:22,143
Wake up!

204
00:10:22,230 --> 00:10:26,016
Hands. Raise 'em high!
You have any weapons?

205
00:10:26,103 --> 00:10:28,758
- No, sir. I don't believe so.
- What about a cell phone?

206
00:10:28,845 --> 00:10:30,804
I believe
I got one in my bag.

207
00:10:30,891 --> 00:10:33,328
Keep your hands up.
I'll find them.

208
00:10:33,415 --> 00:10:34,677
[bag rustling]

209
00:10:34,764 --> 00:10:37,288
You look familiar.
What's your name?

210
00:10:37,375 --> 00:10:38,376
Willie.

211
00:10:40,378 --> 00:10:44,208
- Not Willie Shubert.
- You know this guy?

212
00:10:44,295 --> 00:10:47,603
He's a Bay Area Legend.
I had a bootleg of his first CD.

213
00:10:47,690 --> 00:10:51,128
It was all I listened to
in college.

214
00:10:51,215 --> 00:10:53,087
I always wondered
what happened to you.

215
00:10:58,005 --> 00:10:59,833
Listen up here, Willie.

216
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:03,314
Some of the stuff you're
carrying came off a dead man.

217
00:11:03,401 --> 00:11:05,012
Well, that's not good.

218
00:11:05,099 --> 00:11:07,318
Neither is the dried blood
on your shirt.

219
00:11:07,405 --> 00:11:08,711
'How'd that get there?'

220
00:11:10,452 --> 00:11:11,671
Well, let's see. Uh..

221
00:11:13,281 --> 00:11:16,240
I got the blood on me
when I was stabbing

222
00:11:16,327 --> 00:11:19,940
that fella in the alley.

223
00:11:20,027 --> 00:11:23,247
A trueman, a realman has
but one purpose in this world.

224
00:11:23,334 --> 00:11:25,554
- He lives to fight.
- That's what I'm talking about.

225
00:11:25,641 --> 00:11:27,251
It doesn't matter who he is

226
00:11:27,338 --> 00:11:30,472
what status the world puts,
it's what's in here that counts.

227
00:11:30,559 --> 00:11:32,300
- Mm-hmm.
- Fighting the enemy.

228
00:11:32,387 --> 00:11:33,475
Combating evil.

229
00:11:33,562 --> 00:11:34,998
- Mm-hmm.
- That's nobility.

230
00:11:35,085 --> 00:11:37,087
That's what makes
a man a hero!

231
00:11:37,174 --> 00:11:39,307
Amen, brother.

232
00:11:39,394 --> 00:11:40,874
I'm pretty much done here.

233
00:11:40,961 --> 00:11:42,832
What kinda evil
are you fighting, mostly?

234
00:11:45,574 --> 00:11:48,098
Why do you care?
I don't even know you.

235
00:11:48,185 --> 00:11:51,014
[chuckles]

236
00:11:51,101 --> 00:11:53,495
I don't know you either

237
00:11:53,582 --> 00:11:55,236
but I like you.

238
00:11:55,323 --> 00:11:58,369
Come here.
I wanna show you somethin'.

239
00:11:58,456 --> 00:12:00,067
[alarm chirps]

240
00:12:07,770 --> 00:12:09,598
Check that out.

241
00:12:09,685 --> 00:12:12,732
Remington 700.
That is nice.

242
00:12:12,819 --> 00:12:15,256
You carry this around
in your car?

243
00:12:15,343 --> 00:12:18,433
Never know when
a grizzly bear might appear.

244
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:20,827
Go on. Feel the weight
of that honey.

245
00:12:24,178 --> 00:12:25,788
[clears throat]

246
00:12:29,226 --> 00:12:31,663
Oh.

247
00:12:31,751 --> 00:12:33,361
That's nice.

248
00:12:40,585 --> 00:12:44,154
[Patrick]
'Yeah, makes you wonder,
doesn't it?'

249
00:12:44,241 --> 00:12:47,244
What it'd feel like
to take that shot?

250
00:12:49,769 --> 00:12:52,946
What, on a human being?

251
00:12:53,033 --> 00:12:56,210
We live in a target-rich
environment, my friend.

252
00:12:57,428 --> 00:13:00,823
'It'd be soeasy.'

253
00:13:04,435 --> 00:13:06,350
[chuckles]
Yes, it would.

254
00:13:06,437 --> 00:13:09,005
And you are
a full-on psycho, my friend.

255
00:13:09,092 --> 00:13:12,095
I'm hammered. I'm gonna, um..

256
00:13:12,182 --> 00:13:13,705
Give me that here.

257
00:13:13,793 --> 00:13:16,186
- I'm gonna walk home tonight.
- Get outta here.

258
00:13:16,273 --> 00:13:18,449
Stay frosty.

259
00:13:21,539 --> 00:13:24,107
[inhales deeply]

260
00:13:24,194 --> 00:13:27,415
[exhales deeply]

261
00:13:27,502 --> 00:13:30,461
Let's go. Lisbon's called off
the surveillance on Tibbs.

262
00:13:30,548 --> 00:13:31,985
They think they've found
their killer.

263
00:13:32,072 --> 00:13:34,248
I know I found mine.

264
00:13:40,776 --> 00:13:42,996
I thought I saw something
in Tibbs' eyes yesterday

265
00:13:43,083 --> 00:13:45,476
but I wanted to make sure,
last night I did.

266
00:13:45,563 --> 00:13:46,695
What'd you see?

267
00:13:46,782 --> 00:13:48,566
He's a man on autopilot.

268
00:13:50,177 --> 00:13:51,526
Set to kill.

269
00:13:53,397 --> 00:13:55,835
If we weren't there to stop him
yesterday, I'm confident

270
00:13:55,922 --> 00:13:57,532
he would've pulled the trigger.

271
00:13:57,619 --> 00:14:00,274
- You got that from his eyes?
- Pretty much.

272
00:14:00,361 --> 00:14:03,451
I'm not doubting you.
I'm just...fascinated.

273
00:14:03,538 --> 00:14:06,019
Well, there was the tension
in his body as well

274
00:14:06,106 --> 00:14:09,805
and the overcoat,
but, uh...the eyes were enough.

275
00:14:09,892 --> 00:14:12,329
The trouble is, I mean,
even if you are right

276
00:14:12,416 --> 00:14:14,375
we can't arrest an offender
before he's offended.

277
00:14:14,462 --> 00:14:17,421
It's better than after,
don't you think?

278
00:14:17,508 --> 00:14:19,206
See, here's the problem
with working homicide.

279
00:14:19,293 --> 00:14:20,903
There's so much death.

280
00:14:20,990 --> 00:14:22,949
Tibbs is a man on the verge.

281
00:14:23,036 --> 00:14:26,126
He's a man
on the threshold of murder

282
00:14:26,213 --> 00:14:27,867
possibly even mass murder.

283
00:14:27,954 --> 00:14:30,608
Now who wouldn't wanna try
and stop that kind of nightmare?

284
00:14:30,695 --> 00:14:32,175
Theoretical criminology.

285
00:14:32,262 --> 00:14:34,221
I took that class.
I think it's fascinating.

286
00:14:34,308 --> 00:14:36,701
Uh, it just has
no practical value.

287
00:14:36,788 --> 00:14:39,226
No practical value? I guess the
same could be said

288
00:14:39,313 --> 00:14:41,837
for middle management
bureaucrats.

289
00:14:41,924 --> 00:14:44,318
Chafe as you may, against
the bridle of responsibility

290
00:14:44,405 --> 00:14:46,624
we can't shortchange
due process.

291
00:14:46,711 --> 00:14:48,539
Now, following
a potential offender around

292
00:14:48,626 --> 00:14:50,019
is a waste of resources.

293
00:14:50,106 --> 00:14:51,847
Yeah, you're right.

294
00:14:51,934 --> 00:14:55,155
What we need here
is a confidence trick.

295
00:14:55,242 --> 00:14:56,417
A what?

296
00:14:56,504 --> 00:15:00,073
Scam. Hustle. Bunco. Flimflam.

297
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:03,511
If we can get inside
the world of Henry Tibbs

298
00:15:03,598 --> 00:15:06,079
then I can predict
when and where he will blow.

299
00:15:06,166 --> 00:15:09,865
All we have to do is pop out and
arrest him for attempted murder.

300
00:15:09,952 --> 00:15:12,128
Before we go
too far down this path

301
00:15:12,215 --> 00:15:15,175
I'm not ready to rule out Tibbs
as an actual murder suspect.

302
00:15:15,262 --> 00:15:16,698
'Didn't Willie Shubert confess?'

303
00:15:16,785 --> 00:15:18,700
Yes, he did,
but I don't believe him.

304
00:15:18,787 --> 00:15:20,267
Look at his history.

305
00:15:20,354 --> 00:15:22,878
Mental illness,
substance abuse, vagrancy.

306
00:15:22,965 --> 00:15:25,141
Exactly, and he was covered
in the victim's blood

307
00:15:25,228 --> 00:15:26,708
and in possession
of his belongings.

308
00:15:26,795 --> 00:15:28,928
Yes, b-but there's
no record of violence.

309
00:15:29,015 --> 00:15:31,931
I think that his confession
was a cry for help.

310
00:15:32,018 --> 00:15:34,846
- Willie didn't hurt anyone.
- How do you know that?

311
00:15:34,934 --> 00:15:37,806
You kind of have
to hear him play the saxophone.

312
00:15:38,894 --> 00:15:40,722
I love it, Lisbon.

313
00:15:40,809 --> 00:15:42,550
The beautiful soul defense.

314
00:15:44,378 --> 00:15:46,075
'You two want me believe'

315
00:15:46,162 --> 00:15:48,773
that a man who confessed
to murder did nothing

316
00:15:48,860 --> 00:15:51,211
and a man who did nothing
is a murderer?

317
00:15:51,298 --> 00:15:53,430
You may even be smarter
than you look.

318
00:15:53,517 --> 00:15:55,955
I think Tibbs is a dead end

319
00:15:56,042 --> 00:15:59,828
but I like to reward passion,
so...go prove me wrong.

320
00:15:59,915 --> 00:16:01,873
You have 24 hours
to demonstrate

321
00:16:01,961 --> 00:16:05,529
that Tibbs poses
an imminent danger

322
00:16:05,616 --> 00:16:07,792
and I'm sending
the Shubert case to the DA.

323
00:16:07,879 --> 00:16:10,360
What about my passion?

324
00:16:10,447 --> 00:16:14,060
I called my shot.
Excuse me.

325
00:16:14,147 --> 00:16:17,063
Okay, the goal is
to get close to Tibbs

326
00:16:17,150 --> 00:16:19,935
to figure out
when he will snap.

327
00:16:20,022 --> 00:16:24,200
We're gonna play this...
like a long con.

328
00:16:24,287 --> 00:16:26,811
Rigsby, your objective is
to exploit

329
00:16:26,898 --> 00:16:28,770
the mark's dreams and desires.

330
00:16:28,857 --> 00:16:31,425
Uh, the one thing
we do know about Tibbs..

331
00:16:31,512 --> 00:16:33,035
...he desires a job.

332
00:16:33,122 --> 00:16:34,819
Now according
to Van Pelt's research

333
00:16:34,906 --> 00:16:37,300
he's already gone downtown
and applied for one

334
00:16:37,387 --> 00:16:39,389
at the Goldwyn Insurance Group.

335
00:16:39,476 --> 00:16:40,912
They've rejected
his application

336
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:42,784
but Tibbs doesn't know that yet

337
00:16:42,871 --> 00:16:46,222
so we need the Goldwyn Group
to lend us a meeting room

338
00:16:46,309 --> 00:16:49,312
so we can conduct
a fake job interview.

339
00:16:49,399 --> 00:16:53,403
We also need to know about his family life or lack thereof.

340
00:16:53,490 --> 00:16:56,058
It seems the lady of the house, Linda Tibbs

341
00:16:56,145 --> 00:16:58,060
has filed for a divorce.

342
00:16:58,147 --> 00:16:59,757
We need to know why.

343
00:16:59,844 --> 00:17:02,847
So, Van Pelt, you'll find out where she works

344
00:17:02,934 --> 00:17:04,719
and you will make contact.

345
00:17:04,806 --> 00:17:07,678
Cho, I think it's best
you ditch the race car

346
00:17:07,765 --> 00:17:09,811
and find something
a little more modest

347
00:17:09,898 --> 00:17:12,379
to keep the mark
under surveillance.

348
00:17:12,466 --> 00:17:14,729
Let's have fun.

349
00:17:14,816 --> 00:17:16,035
Good luck to all.

350
00:17:34,662 --> 00:17:36,055
'Your first CD.'

351
00:17:37,447 --> 00:17:40,885
Only CD.
I still have it.

352
00:17:42,887 --> 00:17:46,239
Back in college, whenever
I missed my family in Chicago

353
00:17:46,326 --> 00:17:50,678
I would listen to it.
It helped.

354
00:17:50,765 --> 00:17:53,942
I've been doing
some research on you, Willie.

355
00:17:54,029 --> 00:17:58,120
Not long after you made
this album, you lost your wife.

356
00:17:58,207 --> 00:18:00,253
'Car accident, right?'

357
00:18:00,340 --> 00:18:03,734
You were driving? Is that
why you stopped playing?

358
00:18:03,821 --> 00:18:05,562
What do you want?

359
00:18:05,649 --> 00:18:07,869
Why you confessed to a murder
you didn't commit.

360
00:18:07,956 --> 00:18:12,047
I don't know what you're talking
about. I stuck that boy!

361
00:18:12,134 --> 00:18:14,180
Ready to get
what I got coming.

362
00:18:14,267 --> 00:18:16,486
You need to understand
the reality

363
00:18:16,573 --> 00:18:18,575
of what's going on, Willie.

364
00:18:18,662 --> 00:18:20,403
The DA will put you
in prison.

365
00:18:20,490 --> 00:18:24,755
[scoffs]
I'm old, tired
of sleeping in alleys.

366
00:18:24,842 --> 00:18:27,715
I ain't got no place to go.
Prison's someplace.

367
00:18:27,802 --> 00:18:31,458
And a lethal injection is part
of the vacation package.

368
00:18:32,894 --> 00:18:34,809
You'll get three hots
and a cot

369
00:18:34,896 --> 00:18:36,767
just for stealing off
a dead body.

370
00:18:39,292 --> 00:18:41,511
What do you say
we come clean on this

371
00:18:41,598 --> 00:18:43,557
so we can keep you alive?

372
00:18:46,299 --> 00:18:48,953
Okay, yeah.

373
00:18:50,607 --> 00:18:54,959
Tell me what happened
yesterday.

374
00:18:55,046 --> 00:18:59,138
That fellow that took my photo
earlier in the day had..

375
00:18:59,225 --> 00:19:02,924
...gave me $20
for my photo rights

376
00:19:03,011 --> 00:19:04,795
but when I found him dead

377
00:19:04,882 --> 00:19:07,146
his wallet was laying
on the ground, empty.

378
00:19:07,233 --> 00:19:08,799
Why did you take it?

379
00:19:08,886 --> 00:19:12,716
It's real leather.

380
00:19:12,803 --> 00:19:15,241
And what about the camera?
What happened to that?

381
00:19:15,328 --> 00:19:18,244
I found the cell phone
in his jacket pocket.

382
00:19:18,331 --> 00:19:20,028
I didn't see no camera.

383
00:19:22,335 --> 00:19:26,339
I'm gonna get you out of this,
whether you like it or not.

384
00:19:30,169 --> 00:19:31,779
Thank you.

385
00:19:31,866 --> 00:19:33,346
[receiver clatters]

386
00:19:33,433 --> 00:19:35,696
- You ready? He's here.
- Not really.

387
00:19:35,783 --> 00:19:38,394
Have a seat, please.
Mr. Rigsby will be right in.

388
00:19:38,481 --> 00:19:40,875
He's one
of our senior executives.

389
00:19:42,355 --> 00:19:44,313
'What do you want me
to ask him?'

390
00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:46,272
Ask him whatever you want.
You're charming.

391
00:19:46,359 --> 00:19:47,795
Just get him to open up.

392
00:19:47,882 --> 00:19:49,405
Okay.

393
00:19:49,492 --> 00:19:51,277
The questions
that Agent Wainright gave me?

394
00:19:51,364 --> 00:19:53,844
- Wainright gave you questions?
- Yeah.

395
00:19:53,931 --> 00:19:56,151
He says they're designed
to elicit a patterned response

396
00:19:56,238 --> 00:19:59,067
to determine
antisocial personality disorder.

397
00:19:59,154 --> 00:20:02,418
Well, maybe they'll make you
sound extra-smart just like him.

398
00:20:02,505 --> 00:20:04,812
Okay. Wish me luck.

399
00:20:04,899 --> 00:20:07,249
You'll be fine.
Just don't be yourself.

400
00:20:07,336 --> 00:20:09,295
Whoa. Hang on.
What is that?

401
00:20:09,382 --> 00:20:12,776
That is a large pimple forming
above your brow there.

402
00:20:12,863 --> 00:20:14,343
I don't know. Is there?

403
00:20:14,430 --> 00:20:16,432
Actually, no.
It looks more like a melanoma.

404
00:20:16,519 --> 00:20:19,087
- What? Are you sure?
- No, I'm not.

405
00:20:19,174 --> 00:20:21,916
You should get that checked out
as soon as you can.

406
00:20:22,003 --> 00:20:23,265
- Now run along.
- Mm-hmm.

407
00:20:26,094 --> 00:20:27,530
[door opens]

408
00:20:29,053 --> 00:20:30,359
[closes door]

409
00:20:30,446 --> 00:20:31,795
- Hi.
- Hello, Mr. Tibbs.

410
00:20:31,882 --> 00:20:34,145
Wayne Rigsby.

411
00:20:34,233 --> 00:20:36,104
Named after the duke himself.

412
00:20:36,191 --> 00:20:38,149
Yeah, my dad liked his westerns.

413
00:20:38,237 --> 00:20:39,716
A man after my own heart.

414
00:20:39,803 --> 00:20:41,762
My family's been raising
crops, cattle, and children

415
00:20:41,849 --> 00:20:43,242
in Texas for two centuries.

416
00:20:43,329 --> 00:20:45,896
So, Mr. Tibbs,
uh, what kind of..

417
00:20:45,983 --> 00:20:48,899
'what kind of an employee
would you describe yourself as?'

418
00:20:48,986 --> 00:20:51,424
- Can I get you a drink?
- No, thank you.

419
00:20:51,511 --> 00:20:53,513
I'm here to talk about
your husband Henry.

420
00:20:53,600 --> 00:20:55,297
Grace Van Pelt with the CBI.

421
00:20:55,384 --> 00:20:57,299
[sighs]
What'd he do?

422
00:20:57,386 --> 00:20:58,866
Nothing yet,
but we have reason

423
00:20:58,953 --> 00:21:00,650
to believe that
he might be dangerous.

424
00:21:00,737 --> 00:21:03,392
"Might be"?
That's a good one.

425
00:21:03,479 --> 00:21:05,133
Talk to me.

426
00:21:06,787 --> 00:21:09,268
No, ma'am. I have been
talking for a long time

427
00:21:09,355 --> 00:21:12,488
and I am tired
of nobody listening.

428
00:21:12,575 --> 00:21:14,795
Look, I don't know
who you've been talking to

429
00:21:14,882 --> 00:21:16,362
but I'll take you seriously.

430
00:21:16,449 --> 00:21:18,364
No.

431
00:21:18,451 --> 00:21:21,062
I am not getting lured back
into this again.

432
00:21:21,149 --> 00:21:24,500
I even had a judge turn me away
for being the unstable one.

433
00:21:24,587 --> 00:21:27,938
I'm taking care of myself
from now on.

434
00:21:28,025 --> 00:21:30,376
Rachel, can you cover
this table for me, please?

435
00:21:32,943 --> 00:21:34,684
I have a Master's
in Forensic Psych.

436
00:21:34,771 --> 00:21:36,599
The questions
I gave Rigsby came

437
00:21:36,686 --> 00:21:38,906
from a standardized test
to diagnose psychopathy.

438
00:21:38,993 --> 00:21:41,169
Tricking our mark into taking
a mental health test.

439
00:21:41,256 --> 00:21:43,171
Nice touch.

440
00:21:43,258 --> 00:21:45,913
Just embracing
your spirit of the con.

441
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,525
I cross-referenced Tibbs'
answers with his background.

442
00:21:49,612 --> 00:21:51,832
Do you have any idea
what the test revealed?

443
00:21:51,919 --> 00:21:54,182
No, but I can't wait
to find out.

444
00:21:56,315 --> 00:21:57,620
Nothing.

445
00:21:57,707 --> 00:22:00,580
No evidence of
criminal psychopathy whatsoever.

446
00:22:00,667 --> 00:22:03,278
Tibbs is just
an out-of-work insurance broker.

447
00:22:03,365 --> 00:22:07,064
A monster without conscience,
void of any human empathy.

448
00:22:07,151 --> 00:22:09,980
- Jane.
- Hey, tests can be fooled.

449
00:22:10,067 --> 00:22:13,070
Human behavior never lies.

450
00:22:13,157 --> 00:22:14,855
Normal people,
they can't help but imitate

451
00:22:14,942 --> 00:22:16,378
what happens
in their social world.

452
00:22:16,465 --> 00:22:18,598
One of the most
mimicked behaviors..

453
00:22:18,685 --> 00:22:20,164
...face-touching.

454
00:22:20,251 --> 00:22:23,820
Observe Agent Rigsby,
as neurotic as a border collie.

455
00:22:23,907 --> 00:22:26,127
He can't stop touching his face.

456
00:22:26,214 --> 00:22:30,784
Now Tibbs, his hands never
leave the table, not once.

457
00:22:30,871 --> 00:22:32,916
That's, uh, quite a leap.

458
00:22:33,003 --> 00:22:34,091
Is it?

459
00:22:34,178 --> 00:22:36,224
Look how you're sitting
right now.

460
00:22:36,311 --> 00:22:38,748
You're leaning back with your
legs crossed, just like me.

461
00:22:38,835 --> 00:22:40,794
Oh, please,
but I always sit this way.

462
00:22:40,881 --> 00:22:42,230
You do? Oh.

463
00:22:42,317 --> 00:22:45,320
Hmm. Maybe you're right.

464
00:22:45,407 --> 00:22:48,062
Of course I'm right.

465
00:22:50,107 --> 00:22:51,761
See? You're normal.

466
00:22:51,848 --> 00:22:54,721
That man has the conscience
of a mollusk.

467
00:22:54,808 --> 00:22:56,549
[no audio]

468
00:23:07,821 --> 00:23:09,562
Hey, Tibbs.

469
00:23:21,617 --> 00:23:23,576
- I'm with him.
- Alright. Go ahead.

470
00:23:35,501 --> 00:23:37,894
[engine turns off]

471
00:23:40,462 --> 00:23:41,855
[gunshot]

472
00:23:43,291 --> 00:23:45,206
[gunshots]

473
00:24:08,577 --> 00:24:12,929
[laughs]
Hey. Bad year for Merlot.

474
00:24:13,016 --> 00:24:15,105
'That's my case. You can, uh..'

475
00:24:15,192 --> 00:24:17,368
You can try to hit one,
if you want.

476
00:24:17,456 --> 00:24:18,413
Okay.

477
00:24:33,341 --> 00:24:34,298
Thanks.

478
00:24:34,385 --> 00:24:35,735
No problem.

479
00:24:38,477 --> 00:24:40,261
[machine gunfire]

480
00:24:57,147 --> 00:24:58,758
[cheering]

481
00:25:01,195 --> 00:25:03,153
[indistinct conversations]

482
00:25:12,380 --> 00:25:14,208
Suspect was shooting zombies
with an illegal M240 Bravo

483
00:25:14,295 --> 00:25:16,123
belt-fed, gas-operated.

484
00:25:16,210 --> 00:25:17,777
Zombies?

485
00:25:17,864 --> 00:25:19,518
Lifelike targets. Mannequins.

486
00:25:19,605 --> 00:25:22,259
- No wonder Tibbs' wife left.
- What'd you learn?

487
00:25:22,346 --> 00:25:24,566
She wouldn't talk, but according
to a protective order

488
00:25:24,653 --> 00:25:26,873
she filed in May,
they got into a fight.

489
00:25:26,960 --> 00:25:30,006
He took a hammer to the bedroom.
Blamed her for his failures.

490
00:25:30,093 --> 00:25:32,269
I'm sorry. I don't remember
a protective order.

491
00:25:32,356 --> 00:25:35,403
Judge denied it, said Linda's
request wasn't specific enough

492
00:25:35,490 --> 00:25:36,796
to constitute an emergency.

493
00:25:36,883 --> 00:25:38,101
She's scared of him

494
00:25:38,188 --> 00:25:40,147
but can't get anyone
to take her seriously.

495
00:25:40,234 --> 00:25:42,192
One, Tibbs is living
a life of failure

496
00:25:42,279 --> 00:25:44,978
two, he's socially isolated,
three, he owns firearms

497
00:25:45,065 --> 00:25:48,111
and four,
his wife just left him.

498
00:25:48,198 --> 00:25:50,331
Classic 4-pointed profile
of a mass shooter.

499
00:25:50,418 --> 00:25:52,638
We'll arrest Henry Tibbs
on a felony weapons charge

500
00:25:52,725 --> 00:25:54,988
then we can insist
on counseling.

501
00:25:55,075 --> 00:25:57,730
What happens in a couple months
when he hits the streets?

502
00:25:57,817 --> 00:25:59,209
He reports to parole.

503
00:25:59,296 --> 00:26:01,298
We all know
how effective that is.

504
00:26:01,385 --> 00:26:04,693
- There is no ideal system.
- Sure, there is.

505
00:26:04,780 --> 00:26:06,956
We follow Tibbs,
and catch him in the act

506
00:26:07,043 --> 00:26:08,567
of attempted mass murder.

507
00:26:08,654 --> 00:26:10,960
Jury puts him away for life.
It's that simple.

508
00:26:11,047 --> 00:26:14,355
You are quite the Pied Piper,
Mr. Jane. Quite the salesman.

509
00:26:14,442 --> 00:26:17,140
I happen to think counseling
is our best option right now.

510
00:26:17,227 --> 00:26:20,404
All due respect, chief,
but isn't it worth a shot?

511
00:26:20,491 --> 00:26:23,930
I will not follow around
an almost-bad guy

512
00:26:24,017 --> 00:26:25,584
just waiting for him
to snap.

513
00:26:25,671 --> 00:26:28,978
I vowed to make a prediction,
so predict I shall.

514
00:26:29,065 --> 00:26:32,852
Before the sun sets today,
Henry Tibbs will make his move.

515
00:26:34,288 --> 00:26:35,681
Give us that long.

516
00:26:37,117 --> 00:26:39,032
You have until
the end of the day.

517
00:26:41,295 --> 00:26:42,383
[Cho clears throat]

518
00:26:42,470 --> 00:26:44,690
Is Lisbon always this late?

519
00:26:44,777 --> 00:26:46,213
She had some personal business.

520
00:26:47,214 --> 00:26:48,476
[jingles]

521
00:26:50,478 --> 00:26:51,435
[closes door]

522
00:26:54,787 --> 00:26:56,876
Good morning.
I'm with the CBI.

523
00:26:56,963 --> 00:26:58,660
I need to know
if somebody pawned

524
00:26:58,747 --> 00:27:00,706
a camera here
in the past 24 hours.

525
00:27:00,793 --> 00:27:02,359
'Take a look.'

526
00:27:02,446 --> 00:27:05,841
A high-end DSLR in a case
with multiple lenses.

527
00:27:05,928 --> 00:27:07,930
Oh, yeah.
That one came in last night.

528
00:27:08,017 --> 00:27:11,107
A mangy guy brought it in.
Rotten teeth, dog breath.

529
00:27:13,893 --> 00:27:16,504
That camera is evidence
in a homicide investigation.

530
00:27:16,591 --> 00:27:18,593
- Where is it at now?
- Sold it.

531
00:27:18,680 --> 00:27:20,595
Do you have a record
of the purchase?

532
00:27:23,206 --> 00:27:25,513
Alright. How about
the memory card? You sell that?

533
00:27:25,600 --> 00:27:27,907
I sell those separately.

534
00:27:27,994 --> 00:27:29,865
More money for me.

535
00:27:29,952 --> 00:27:32,476
That one had a 32 gig card.

536
00:27:34,827 --> 00:27:36,742
I need a working digital camera.

537
00:27:36,829 --> 00:27:37,743
Sure.

538
00:27:41,268 --> 00:27:43,836
Thirty bucks.

539
00:27:43,923 --> 00:27:45,925
I'll give you five
for a two-minute rental.

540
00:28:01,201 --> 00:28:02,724
[Lisbon]
'Do you know this man?'

541
00:28:02,811 --> 00:28:05,945
[Willie chuckles]
Mike Flynn.

542
00:28:06,032 --> 00:28:09,731
Anger management issues.

543
00:28:09,818 --> 00:28:12,168
And I know where he keeps
his bedroll, too.

544
00:28:15,432 --> 00:28:18,697
- Mike Flynn?
- Get the hell out of my house!

545
00:28:20,176 --> 00:28:21,874
You are Mike Flynn, right?

546
00:28:21,961 --> 00:28:24,267
And y-you're
in a man's house, and I..

547
00:28:24,354 --> 00:28:26,313
I'll call the police
if you don't go.

548
00:28:26,400 --> 00:28:27,357
- Just calm down.
- Now!

549
00:28:27,444 --> 00:28:28,881
Calm down.
We are the police.

550
00:28:28,968 --> 00:28:30,360
[panting]

551
00:28:30,447 --> 00:28:32,101
Sushi for lunch?

552
00:28:32,188 --> 00:28:34,887
Expensive
goose-down sleeping bag?

553
00:28:34,974 --> 00:28:36,845
How'd you pay for this stuff?

554
00:28:38,107 --> 00:28:39,761
The streets have been
kind lately.

555
00:28:39,848 --> 00:28:41,981
We need to check
your backpack, okay?

556
00:28:49,118 --> 00:28:51,512
- Put it down!
- Hey. Take it easy.

557
00:28:51,599 --> 00:28:52,687
Drop it.

558
00:28:53,340 --> 00:28:54,471
Put it down.

559
00:29:00,390 --> 00:29:03,176
I have a photo of you coming
after a murder victim

560
00:29:03,263 --> 00:29:06,919
with that knife. You wanna
explain that, Mr. Flynn?

561
00:29:07,006 --> 00:29:08,834
I-I don't like having
my photo taken.

562
00:29:08,921 --> 00:29:11,097
[cell phone ringing]
Get your hands
behind your back.

563
00:29:11,184 --> 00:29:12,838
[Rigsby]
'Now.'

564
00:29:12,925 --> 00:29:14,448
[ringing continues]

565
00:29:14,535 --> 00:29:16,537
- Lisbon.
- 'It's Cho.'

566
00:29:16,624 --> 00:29:20,236
'I just lost Tibbs on
the freeway. He's off the grid.'

567
00:29:20,323 --> 00:29:22,673
Should we be worried?

568
00:29:22,761 --> 00:29:25,633
What was Tibbs doing
before Cho lost sight of him?

569
00:29:25,720 --> 00:29:27,461
Driving around the city,
aimless.

570
00:29:27,548 --> 00:29:30,116
As predicted, he's trying
to work up the courage.

571
00:29:30,203 --> 00:29:32,466
Can't even put out
a BOLO to find him.

572
00:29:32,553 --> 00:29:35,512
We can pick him on a weapons
charge for the machine gun.

573
00:29:35,599 --> 00:29:37,384
Oh. Whoa.

574
00:29:37,471 --> 00:29:40,474
You promised. Sunset. If
I can get inside his house

575
00:29:40,561 --> 00:29:43,042
I know I can figure out
where Tibbs will go.

576
00:29:43,129 --> 00:29:45,566
You'll never get a judge
to grant you a warrant.

577
00:29:45,653 --> 00:29:47,437
'Wait a minute.
We don't need one.'

578
00:29:47,524 --> 00:29:49,483
All we need is permission
from a homeowner.

579
00:29:51,093 --> 00:29:53,704
I already told you,
I'm not getting involved.

580
00:29:53,792 --> 00:29:56,533
We're not asking you to.
We need for you to cooperate.

581
00:29:56,620 --> 00:30:00,233
It doesn't matter. I don't trust
you, and I'm not gonna help.

582
00:30:00,320 --> 00:30:03,845
If you quit now, you're gonna
miss the best part. Payback.

583
00:30:03,932 --> 00:30:05,151
What are you talking about?

584
00:30:05,238 --> 00:30:10,199
A few months ago,
I had a fiance who..

585
00:30:10,286 --> 00:30:12,506
- Well, he tried to kill me.
- You're lying.

586
00:30:12,593 --> 00:30:14,551
Look, everybody thought
he was the greatest guy

587
00:30:14,638 --> 00:30:18,120
including me,
until he pulled a gun.

588
00:30:18,207 --> 00:30:19,426
What did you do?

589
00:30:19,513 --> 00:30:20,862
I shot him.

590
00:30:22,733 --> 00:30:23,691
Dead.

591
00:30:28,870 --> 00:30:32,526
[inhales deeply]
What can I do to help?

592
00:30:32,613 --> 00:30:34,006
We need to search your house.

593
00:30:44,190 --> 00:30:48,020
- Nice.
- Well, it's a choice.

594
00:30:48,107 --> 00:30:50,065
Lacks a woman's touch.

595
00:30:58,813 --> 00:31:00,423
Got something.

596
00:31:06,081 --> 00:31:07,256
[Patrick]
'Wow.'

597
00:31:07,343 --> 00:31:10,607
The imagination station.

598
00:31:14,307 --> 00:31:16,048
"All that's necessary
for evil to triumph

599
00:31:16,135 --> 00:31:17,658
'is that good men
do nothing."'

600
00:31:17,745 --> 00:31:20,356
That's, uh, Edmund Burke,
I believe.

601
00:31:24,621 --> 00:31:27,624
[Lisbon]
'Wherever Tibbs is, at least
he doesn't have these with him.'

602
00:31:27,711 --> 00:31:30,497
Well, he may have
other weapons.

603
00:31:32,238 --> 00:31:34,414
Check this out.

604
00:31:34,501 --> 00:31:37,504
[Lisbon]
'The mayor's public appearance
schedule.'

605
00:31:37,591 --> 00:31:40,072
I wonder what he did
for Tibbs to target him.

606
00:31:40,159 --> 00:31:43,118
Doesn't matter. All Tibbs
cares about is the rush

607
00:31:43,205 --> 00:31:44,946
that comes from killing
an important man.

608
00:31:45,033 --> 00:31:47,862
The mayor's power
becomes his power.

609
00:31:47,949 --> 00:31:50,169
[Lisbon]
'Where is Tibbs now?'

610
00:31:50,256 --> 00:31:53,433
'Who else do you think would be
a target for his rage?'

611
00:31:53,520 --> 00:31:58,568
I don't know, but he hasn't
signed his divorce papers yet.

612
00:31:58,655 --> 00:32:01,354
Do we have someone at the diner
where Tibbs' wife works?

613
00:32:05,488 --> 00:32:07,447
[door closes]

614
00:32:07,534 --> 00:32:10,058
Where is she?
Where's my wife?

615
00:32:11,581 --> 00:32:13,279
Duffel bag could be a weapon.

616
00:32:13,366 --> 00:32:15,368
- 'Where's my wife?'
- We need to hold back.

617
00:32:15,455 --> 00:32:18,153
We move too soon, we blow
our whole cover with Tibbs.

618
00:32:18,240 --> 00:32:21,069
- Can I help you?
- I don't know, man.

619
00:32:21,156 --> 00:32:23,071
You helping my wife
hide from me?

620
00:32:23,158 --> 00:32:25,030
Linda went home early today.

621
00:32:25,117 --> 00:32:28,033
[chuckles]
She didn't go home.

622
00:32:28,120 --> 00:32:31,253
She hasn't been home in weeks.

623
00:32:31,340 --> 00:32:34,430
She staying with you?
You can tell me.

624
00:32:34,517 --> 00:32:36,606
You hot for my wife?

625
00:32:36,693 --> 00:32:38,826
You bangin'my wife?

626
00:32:38,913 --> 00:32:41,568
[sighs]
I need you to go now.

627
00:32:41,655 --> 00:32:44,223
Please? You're scaring
my customers.

628
00:32:49,228 --> 00:32:50,925
Okay, I'll go.

629
00:32:51,012 --> 00:32:53,232
Next time you see Linda..

630
00:32:53,319 --> 00:32:55,277
...give her something from me.

631
00:32:55,364 --> 00:32:56,713
[unzips]

632
00:33:01,196 --> 00:33:02,981
Tell her to choke on these.

633
00:33:10,075 --> 00:33:12,468
Well, at least
you didn't shoot this one.

634
00:33:18,257 --> 00:33:19,867
If I understand
this correctly

635
00:33:19,954 --> 00:33:22,913
two of my agents almost shot
a man in a crowded diner

636
00:33:23,001 --> 00:33:24,480
for bringing his wife
flowers.

637
00:33:24,567 --> 00:33:26,439
"Almost" is the key word there.

638
00:33:26,526 --> 00:33:29,442
I've listened.
I've empathized.

639
00:33:29,529 --> 00:33:31,052
I've heard enough.

640
00:33:31,139 --> 00:33:33,402
Case is too dangerous.
We need a new approach.

641
00:33:33,489 --> 00:33:35,317
Agreed. No more sitting around
and waiting

642
00:33:35,404 --> 00:33:37,363
for something to push Tibbs
over the edge.

643
00:33:37,450 --> 00:33:39,191
We need to create
our own stressor.

644
00:33:39,278 --> 00:33:42,020
Are you saying, incite
the suspect to violence?

645
00:33:42,107 --> 00:33:44,805
It wouldn't take much. Cut off
the power to his house

646
00:33:44,892 --> 00:33:46,589
a couple of eggs on his truck.

647
00:33:46,676 --> 00:33:49,201
Enough to make him go berserk,
do something violent.

648
00:33:49,288 --> 00:33:50,898
You're off the case, Jane.

649
00:33:50,985 --> 00:33:53,770
Actually, as a matter of fact,
there is no case.

650
00:33:53,857 --> 00:33:57,600
Henry Tibbs is a sick man,
and he needs psychological help.

651
00:33:57,687 --> 00:33:59,559
What if he can't be helped?

652
00:33:59,646 --> 00:34:02,953
Weare the criminals then,
if we don't at least try.

653
00:34:03,041 --> 00:34:07,436
- I'm taking Rigsby and Cho.
- Where?

654
00:34:07,523 --> 00:34:10,265
I'm arresting Henry Tibbs
on felony weapons charges.

655
00:34:12,833 --> 00:34:16,054
I wonder if our new boss
is in a little over his head.

656
00:34:16,141 --> 00:34:17,490
Really? I don't.

657
00:34:17,577 --> 00:34:18,665
Tibbs could kill him.

658
00:34:18,752 --> 00:34:20,710
Or he could save Tibbs.

659
00:34:31,330 --> 00:34:34,115
Okay, you two,
cover the back.

660
00:34:39,903 --> 00:34:42,819
I'm sick of watching everyone
play with this man's life.

661
00:34:44,560 --> 00:34:45,605
Yeah?

662
00:34:50,088 --> 00:34:52,351
You're with
the Goldwyn Group, right?

663
00:34:52,438 --> 00:34:54,744
Is this about
the job interview?

664
00:34:54,831 --> 00:34:57,356
No, sir.
I'm afraid it's not.

665
00:34:57,443 --> 00:35:00,315
Hi, Henry.
My name is Luther Wainright.

666
00:35:02,796 --> 00:35:04,885
Hold on.
You-you were at CBI.

667
00:35:04,972 --> 00:35:07,366
That's right.
We are with the government.

668
00:35:07,453 --> 00:35:09,629
We've been watching you.

669
00:35:09,716 --> 00:35:10,804
You what?

670
00:35:10,891 --> 00:35:12,501
We spoke with your wife,
Henry.

671
00:35:12,588 --> 00:35:15,939
We know you're in a lot of pain
and we're worried about you.

672
00:35:16,026 --> 00:35:17,637
The government
would like to help.

673
00:35:17,724 --> 00:35:20,727
Wait. Wait, wait.
What the hell is going on?

674
00:35:20,814 --> 00:35:23,686
Look, I know this is all
pretty confusing

675
00:35:23,773 --> 00:35:25,384
and we're gonna explain
everything.

676
00:35:25,471 --> 00:35:27,429
First, however, we need
to search your house.

677
00:35:27,516 --> 00:35:30,824
- I have a warrant right here.
- You don't need a warrant.

678
00:35:30,911 --> 00:35:34,958
You can come inside my house.
I got nothing to hide.

679
00:35:35,045 --> 00:35:37,570
- Thank you.
- Oh. Excuse me, sir.

680
00:35:37,657 --> 00:35:39,963
We're busting in.
Cover the rear exit.

681
00:35:40,050 --> 00:35:41,269
Hold the door.

682
00:35:41,356 --> 00:35:42,705
[engine revving]

683
00:35:48,494 --> 00:35:50,887
Hey. This is Wainright.
Tibbs is on the run.

684
00:35:50,974 --> 00:35:53,238
Get us a bird in the air.

685
00:35:53,325 --> 00:35:56,502
Hey, Jane. Tibbs evaded arrest.
He's on the move.

686
00:36:00,506 --> 00:36:01,420
Okay.

687
00:36:29,578 --> 00:36:30,710
You.

688
00:36:30,797 --> 00:36:32,190
Hey, Henry.

689
00:36:34,279 --> 00:36:35,323
[crowd gasps]

690
00:36:35,410 --> 00:36:37,891
Okay. Stay calm. Don't move.

691
00:36:37,978 --> 00:36:40,676
Do not draw your weapons.
This man will not hurt you.

692
00:36:40,763 --> 00:36:43,375
He cannot hurt you.

693
00:36:44,854 --> 00:36:45,855
No.

694
00:36:45,942 --> 00:36:47,596
Isn't that right, Henry?

695
00:36:47,683 --> 00:36:50,773
What the hell are you
talking about?

696
00:36:50,860 --> 00:36:54,342
You don't wanna pull that
trigger. You think you do.

697
00:36:54,429 --> 00:36:57,127
You think it'll switch off
that vacancy sign in your soul

698
00:36:57,215 --> 00:37:00,566
but the truth is, taking
a life's just gonna make things

699
00:37:00,653 --> 00:37:05,135
a whole lot more complicated.
Believe me.

700
00:37:05,223 --> 00:37:07,660
I don't expect to live
past today.

701
00:37:07,747 --> 00:37:10,097
But you can.
You haven't hurt anyone yet.

702
00:37:10,184 --> 00:37:11,707
You put that gun away now

703
00:37:11,794 --> 00:37:15,233
and we will get you
the help that you need.

704
00:37:15,320 --> 00:37:17,104
I think..

705
00:37:19,237 --> 00:37:21,630
I think it's too late for me..

706
00:37:23,502 --> 00:37:25,373
...and everyone here.

707
00:37:25,460 --> 00:37:28,158
[screaming]

708
00:37:28,246 --> 00:37:29,856
Hold on, boss.

709
00:37:42,912 --> 00:37:45,045
Whew.

710
00:37:45,132 --> 00:37:47,308
Loud.

711
00:37:47,395 --> 00:37:50,485
Hey. How ya doin'?

712
00:37:50,572 --> 00:37:53,053
You're under arrest
for the attempted murder of..

713
00:37:53,140 --> 00:37:56,012
...everybody. Turn around.
Put your hands on your head.

714
00:37:56,099 --> 00:37:57,710
[handcuffs rattle]

715
00:38:03,193 --> 00:38:05,195
What's going on?
How come no one's hurt?

716
00:38:05,283 --> 00:38:06,762
When we searched Tibbs' house

717
00:38:06,849 --> 00:38:09,069
we replaced all of
his live rounds with blanks

718
00:38:09,156 --> 00:38:10,897
in case we lost him again.

719
00:38:12,681 --> 00:38:14,509
So long, partner.

720
00:38:14,596 --> 00:38:15,945
Let's go.

721
00:38:19,427 --> 00:38:22,387
The DA is offering you
90 days in county jail

722
00:38:22,474 --> 00:38:26,216
if you plead no contest to two
counts of theft from a body.

723
00:38:26,304 --> 00:38:28,262
You'll have a warm bed
for the winter

724
00:38:28,349 --> 00:38:31,134
and shelter waiting for you
when you get out.

725
00:38:31,221 --> 00:38:32,832
Sounds good.

726
00:38:35,704 --> 00:38:37,532
Thanks.

727
00:38:37,619 --> 00:38:41,275
Oh, um, I was
in the pawnshop today

728
00:38:41,362 --> 00:38:43,146
and I picked something up
I thought

729
00:38:43,233 --> 00:38:45,671
maybe you could use
when you get out.

730
00:38:52,373 --> 00:38:54,897
I haven't played
one of these in years.

731
00:38:54,984 --> 00:38:56,943
I didn't think so.

732
00:38:57,030 --> 00:39:00,076
Your lawyer's gonna be here
in a couple of minutes.

733
00:39:00,163 --> 00:39:02,862
You take of yourself,
Willie.

734
00:39:04,994 --> 00:39:06,300
[blinds rattle]

735
00:39:12,393 --> 00:39:14,787
[saxophone music]

736
00:39:34,241 --> 00:39:36,678
Uh..

737
00:39:36,765 --> 00:39:40,290
That's, uh, that's
kind of...my spot.

738
00:39:40,378 --> 00:39:41,944
Oh.

739
00:39:42,031 --> 00:39:45,383
- Right. Sorry.
- Yeah.

740
00:39:45,470 --> 00:39:48,429
- Thank you.
- Mm-hmm.

741
00:39:48,516 --> 00:39:49,909
[inhales deeply and sighs]

742
00:39:52,955 --> 00:39:56,045
You, uh, set me up today.

743
00:39:56,132 --> 00:40:00,136
You knew I would be the stressor
that pushed Tibbs over the edge.

744
00:40:00,223 --> 00:40:02,965
Well, one can never
really know these things.

745
00:40:03,052 --> 00:40:05,011
To be honest,
I was rooting for you.

746
00:40:05,098 --> 00:40:06,665
- Really?
- Yes.

747
00:40:06,752 --> 00:40:09,319
I find your, uh,
youthful brand of earnestness

748
00:40:09,407 --> 00:40:10,973
quite refreshing.

749
00:40:11,060 --> 00:40:13,759
How did you know Tibbs was
gonna go to that garden?

750
00:40:13,846 --> 00:40:15,325
Educated guess.

751
00:40:15,413 --> 00:40:18,154
He had it circled
in his calendar.

752
00:40:18,241 --> 00:40:21,331
Well, then why on earth wouldn't
you clear the area first?

753
00:40:21,419 --> 00:40:24,465
The plan that was approved
by, uh...you

754
00:40:24,552 --> 00:40:30,471
was to catch Tibbs
in the act, I believe.

755
00:40:33,343 --> 00:40:35,389
You know that psychopathy test
I gave Tibbs?

756
00:40:35,476 --> 00:40:37,217
Mm.

757
00:40:37,304 --> 00:40:39,828
I figured out
why it didn't work.

758
00:40:39,915 --> 00:40:42,265
See, it's a rating scale
based on observation

759
00:40:42,352 --> 00:40:44,572
so the more time you spend
with the subject

760
00:40:44,659 --> 00:40:46,705
the more accurately you can
then observe

761
00:40:46,792 --> 00:40:49,055
their behavioral traits

762
00:40:49,142 --> 00:40:52,537
and, uh, guess what
I've been doing?

763
00:40:52,624 --> 00:40:54,539
Oh, you haven't.

764
00:40:56,192 --> 00:40:58,934
Glibness
and superficial charm?

765
00:40:59,021 --> 00:41:02,068
- Check.
- You flatter me.

766
00:41:02,155 --> 00:41:05,593
- Grandiose sense of self-worth?
- You betcha.

767
00:41:05,680 --> 00:41:09,031
'Cunning and manipulative,
poor behavioral controls'

768
00:41:09,118 --> 00:41:12,600
failure to accept responsibility
for own actions.

769
00:41:12,687 --> 00:41:15,690
So I, uh, I cross-referenced
all of my observations

770
00:41:15,777 --> 00:41:19,651
with your history as a thief
and a con artist

771
00:41:19,738 --> 00:41:23,785
and, uh,
congratulations, Jane.

772
00:41:23,872 --> 00:41:26,048
'According to the score'

773
00:41:26,135 --> 00:41:30,357
you are a clinical psychopath.

774
00:41:30,444 --> 00:41:32,272
Wow.

775
00:41:32,359 --> 00:41:35,144
Well...certainly explains a lot.

776
00:41:36,537 --> 00:41:38,234
Now I know what
I'm dealing with

777
00:41:38,321 --> 00:41:40,672
so I'll adjust accordingly.

778
00:41:40,759 --> 00:41:42,151
As you should.

779
00:41:47,156 --> 00:41:48,418
Lisbon.

780
00:41:51,857 --> 00:41:53,772
Everything alright?

781
00:41:53,859 --> 00:41:55,948
Uh, yeah. Yeah.

782
00:41:56,035 --> 00:41:57,950
I like our new boss.

783
00:41:58,037 --> 00:41:59,908
I think we'll work
very well together.

784
00:42:01,954 --> 00:42:03,651
Would you like some tea?

785
00:42:03,738 --> 00:42:05,348
I'm tired.
I'm gonna go home.

786
00:42:06,611 --> 00:42:08,743
I made a pot.

787
00:42:08,830 --> 00:42:11,267
Alright.
I'll take one to go.

788
00:42:11,354 --> 00:42:13,574
I heard you got your man.

789
00:42:13,661 --> 00:42:16,882
As did you. Nice work.

790
00:42:16,969 --> 00:42:19,275
You think I'm
an antisocial personality?

791
00:42:19,362 --> 00:42:21,364
Yeah. Sure. Who isn't?

792
00:42:25,020 --> 00:42:27,675
Stay tuned for scenes
from our next episode.

